AURIX blinky examples
=====================

Minimal bare-metal LED-blink images for three Infineon AURIX Lite Kits,
used to check end to end that OpenOCD can erase, program and verify the
program flash of an AURIX device, and that the programmed image really
runs from a cold boot with no debugger attached. Each board variant also
doubles as the firmware image for the shared hardware regression suite
in common/.

    tc27x/    AURIX TC275 Lite Kit  (TC2x family)
    tc375/    AURIX TC375 Lite Kit  (TC3x family)
    tc4d7/    AURIX TC4D7 Lite Kit  (TC4x family)
    common/   Hardware regression suite shared by all three boards

See each subfolder's own README for that board's build, flash and notes.
Start with tc375/README for the most heavily commented walk-through
(the first board this support was brought up on); the other two READMEs
focus on what differs for their family.

If a board stops responding or shows a fault LED, see RECOVERY.md and
recover-board.sh in this directory -- a self-contained runbook/script to
get it back to a known-good, blinking state using only this repo's own
OpenOCD build.

Hardware regression suite
--------------------------

common/test-aurix-hardware.cfg is a single OpenOCD-script suite that
exercises memory access, flash erase/program/verify, reset/halt/resume/
step, breakpoints, watchpoints and sustained transfers against real
hardware, then finishes by programming that family's blinky image and
sampling the port register to confirm the LEDs really alternate.
Sections 1-17 are family-neutral; running it against the TC2x or TC4x
kits instead of its native TC375 needs a thin per-family wrapper that
sets a handful of documented overrides (TARGET, PFLASH_BANKS, flash test
address, DMU register offsets, and so on) and then sources the shared
file:

    common/test-aurix-hardware.cfg          native TC375 suite
    common/test-aurix-hardware-tc2x.cfg     TC2x (TC275) wrapper
    common/test-aurix-hardware-tc4d7.cfg    TC4x (TC4D7) wrapper

Each wrapper's own header comment documents exactly which overrides it
sets and why, including hardware-confirmed register addresses and the
Infineon manual sections they were cross-checked against. Run a suite
from inside common/, e.g.:

    openocd -f board/infineon/kit_a2g_tc375_lite.cfg \
            -f test-aurix-hardware.cfg

    openocd -f board/infineon/kit_a1g_tc275_lite.cfg \
            -f test-aurix-hardware-tc2x.cfg

    openocd -f board/infineon/kit_a3g_tc4d7_lite.cfg \
            -f test-aurix-hardware-tc4d7.cfg

Each check prints PASS or FAIL, the suite prints a final tally and exits
non zero if anything failed, so it can be used directly as a pass/fail
gate. It is deliberately not wired into "make check": it needs a
specific board attached over USB, so it must never run as part of an
ordinary build. The hardware-free tests for the AURIX support live in
testing/tcl_commands and do run under "make check".
